[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to pytest"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"pytest is a powerful and flexible testing framework for Python. It simplifies writing and executing tests, offering features like auto-discovery of test functions, detailed failure reports, and extensive plugin support. This tutorial will guide you through the basics of pytest and demonstrate how to write effective tests."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you begin, ensure you have Python installed. Then, install pytest using pip:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pip install pytest"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing Your First Test"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"pytest automatically discovers test functions in files named 'test_*.py' or '*_test.py'. Test functions should start with 'test_'. Let's create a simple test file named 'test_example.py':"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"# test_example.py

def add(x, y):
    return x + y


def test_add():
    assert add(2, 3) == 5
    assert add(-1, 1) == 0
    assert add(0, 0) == 0"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Running Tests"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run the tests, navigate to the directory containing 'test_example.py' in your terminal and execute:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"pytest"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"pytest will discover and run all test functions in the current directory and its subdirectories. The output will show the test results, including any failures."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Assertions"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"pytest uses standard Python 'assert' statements to verify expectations. If an assertion fails, pytest will provide detailed information about the failure, including the values involved."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Fixtures"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Fixtures are functions that provide a fixed baseline for tests. They can be used to set up resources, data, or states that are needed by multiple tests. Here's an example:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import pytest

@pytest.fixture
def example_data():
    return [1, 2, 3]


def test_example_data(example_data):
    assert len(example_data) == 3
    assert example_data[0] == 1"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'example_data' is a fixture that returns a list. The 'test_example_data' function takes 'example_data' as an argument, and pytest automatically injects the result of the fixture function."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Parametrization"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"pytest allows you to run the same test function with different inputs using parametrization. This is useful for testing a function with a variety of edge cases or input values."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import pytest

@pytest.mark.parametrize("input, expected", [
    (2, 4),
    (3, 9),
    (4, 16),
])
def test_square(input, expected):
    assert input * input == expected"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The '@pytest.mark.parametrize' decorator specifies the input parameters and their expected outputs. pytest will run the 'test_square' function once for each set of parameters."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Markers"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Markers are used to categorize and filter tests. You can use built-in markers or define your own. For example, you can mark a test as slow:"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import pytest

@pytest.mark.slow
def test_slow_function():
    # This test takes a long time to run
    import time
    time.sleep(2)"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can then run only the slow tests using the '-m' option:"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"pytest -m slow"},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Plugins"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"pytest has a rich ecosystem of plugins that extend its functionality. Plugins can add new features, change the way tests are run, or provide additional reporting. Some popular plugins include 'pytest-cov' for coverage reporting and 'pytest-django' for testing Django applications."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial has covered the basics of pytest, including installation, writing tests, assertions, fixtures, parametrization, markers, and plugins. pytest is a versatile testing framework that can help you write robust and maintainable tests for your Python projects. Explore the pytest documentation for more advanced features and options."}]
